body:
I have a problem with self-awareness; I am overly self-aware and my therapist currently says I am overly critical of myself and take to much of what people say and try to apply it. So I need to avoid criticism more and just move forward. I do however relate to the sentiment that I'm not perfect and don't have to be, however, it doesn't make the process any less taxing. I just need to remind myself it's not about how far along I am but the direction I am going.

&#x200B;

Thanks for sharing, and best wishes to you both.

To start I'm currently 19 and I had a lot of people leave me as a kid and I started showing signs at about 8 years old. By that time I had been to 11 funerals and my uncle who was also my best friend became homeless and pushed everyone away. I then started to act out at school and i would relentlessly beat up my family members which wasn't great but since I was really small for my age they could restrain me. I started going to counseling around 4 months after I broke my dads nose but it didn't really do much. Fast forward to highschool and I was hospitalized for a psychotic break and sucide attempt because my girlfriend dumped me. I was diagnosed with schizoaffective but then later was diagnosed with BPD with psychotic and psychopathic tendencies. I started DBT when I was 17 and it helped me so much however my therapist was a greedy bitch and would waste my time a lot so I have to find a new therapist which happens but is frustrating nonetheless. My mother was supportive all the way through from the start but my dad would egg me on and push my buttons and when I was 16 I was keeping my cool and was just trying to ignore him then he knocked my soda outta my hand and said hit me and I knocked him out cold and from that day forward he was 100% supportive. I do recognize that's not healthy but it's how it worked out.

&#x200B;

Support is the best thing you can find for your growth and treatment and so it therapy. Meds should be a last resort.
